            "description": "<html><body><figure class=\"getty-figure\" data-type=\"getty-image\"><picture> <img alt=\"Yield Curve\" data-id=\"1404937730\" data-type=\"getty-image\" fetchpriority=\"high\" height=\"960\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 132px), (max-width: 1199px) calc(100vw - 666px), (max-width: 1307px) calc(100vw - 708px), 600px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1404937730/image_1404937730.jpg?io=getty-c-w630\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1404937730/image_1404937730.jpg?io=getty-c-w1536 1536w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1404937730/image_1404937730.jpg?io=getty-c-w1280 1280w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1404937730/image_1404937730.jpg?io=getty-c-w1080 1080w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1404937730/image_1404937730.jpg?io=getty-c-w750 750w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1404937730/image_1404937730.jpg?io=getty-c-w640 640w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1404937730/image_1404937730.jpg?io=getty-c-w480 480w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1404937730/image_1404937730.jpg?io=getty-c-w320 320w, https://static.seekingalpha.com/cdn/s3/uploads/getty_images/1404937730/image_1404937730.jpg?io=getty-c-w240 240w\" width=\"1536\"/> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\"> </p> <p class=\"item-credits\">Torsten Asmus/iStock via Getty Images</p></figcaption></figure><h3>Fast Facts About the Quadratic Interest Rate Volatility and Inflation Hedge ETF</h3> <p data-eci=\"true\">The <strong>Quadratic Interest Rate Volatility and Inflation Hedge ETF (<span class=\"ticker-hover-wrapper\"><a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/IVOL\" title=\"Quadratic Interest Rate Vltly and Infltn Hdg ETF\">IVOL</a></span>)</strong> is an actively managed income fund launched on May 13, 2019. It is a hedge fund-like ETF with three objectives:</p> <ol> <li>Provide inflation-protected income,</li> <li> <span>Pr</span>ofit from a steepening yield curve (when long-term rates rise relative to short-term rates),</li> <li>Profit from market stress when rate volatility increases.</li> </ol> <p>IVOL has a 12-month trailing yield of 3.92% and a net expense ratio of 0.98%. Distributions are paid monthly. It is a small yet liquid ETF, with about $212 million in assets under management and $3.4 million in average daily volume. <a href=\"https://quadraticllc.com/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://quadraticllc.com/\">Quadratic Capital Management</a> is a small asset management firm focused on innovative interest-rate strategies using OTC options. Its main products are IVOL and the Quadratic Deflation ETF (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/BNDD\" title=\"Quadratic Deflation ETF\">BNDD</a>).</p> <h3>The Strategy of IVOL</h3> <p>IVOL primarily invests in Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (“TIPS”) and hedges them against relative variations of interest rates (i.e., variations in the slope of the U.S. Treasury interest rate curve). TIPS are securities issued by the U.S. Treasury that have principal and interest payments linked to inflation measured by the Consumer Price Index (“CPI”).</p> <p>TIPS exposure may be obtained through ETFs such as the Schwab U.S. TIPS ETF (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/SCHP\" title=\"Schwab US TIPS ETF\">SCHP</a>), which tracks the Bloomberg US Treasury Inflation-Linked Bond Index. As described by Schwab, SCHP invests in Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities with at least one year remaining to maturity and $500 million of outstanding face value. IVOL is not definitely linked to SCHP. It may invest in similar ETFs or buy TIPS directly. Additionally, it holds options tied to the shape of the U.S. interest rate curve. The options strategy is designed to generate positive returns when the spread between the interest rates of long-term and short-term Treasuries widens or when interest rate volatility increases. This typically happens in an inflationary environment or in a stock market correction. Therefore, IVOL is primarily designed as a hedge against inflation and market downturns. Targeted options are tied to the shape of the U.S. interest rate swap curve and traded in the over-the-counter market, with a time to expiration usually between six months and three years. The fund only takes long positions and intends to invest less than 20% of total assets in options.</p> <h3>Portfolio</h3> <p>Portfolio characteristics are an example from October 22, 2026. They may have changed by the time you read this.</p> <p>SCHP represents 72.7% of asset value. IVOL also holds cash instruments (15.8%) and is long in nine options (11.5% in aggregate), all based on the 10-year to 2-year interest rate spread. Expiration dates span from November 2026 to October 2029.</p> <div class=\"table-responsive\">\n<span class=\"sa-table-scroll-wrapper sa-hide-scrollbar\"><span data-intersection-boundary=\"start\" data-test-id=\"table-scroll-wrapper-boundary-start\"></span><table> <colgroup> <col/> <col/> <col/> </colgroup> <tr> <td><p>Holding</p></td> <td><p>Type</p></td> <td><p>%Weight</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>SCHP</p></td> <td><p>ETF</p></td> <td><p>72.74</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>Cash</p></td> <td><p>Cash</p></td> <td><p>15.79</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>USD CMS 2-10 06/15/27 3</p></td> <td><p>Option</p></td> <td><p>2.74</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>USD CMS 2-10 10/18/29 38</p></td> <td><p>Option</p></td> <td><p>2.61</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>USD CMS 2-10 05/17/28 55</p></td> <td><p>Option</p></td> <td><p>2.02</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>USD CMS 2-10 09/15/28 85</p></td> <td><p>Option</p></td> <td><p>1.58</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>USD CMS 2-10 04/14/27 20</p></td> <td><p>Option</p></td> <td><p>1.06</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>USD CMS 2-10 09/15/27 50</p></td> <td><p>Option</p></td> <td><p>0.55</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>USD CMS 2-10 01/13/27 1</p></td> <td><p>Option</p></td> <td><p>0.47</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>USD CMS 2-10 02/08/27 13</p></td> <td><p>Option</p></td> <td><p>0.44</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>USD CMS 2-10 11/13/26 43</p></td> <td><p>Option</p></td> <td><p>0</p></td> </tr> </table>\n<span data-intersection-boundary=\"end\" data-test-id=\"table-scroll-wrapper-boundary-end\"></span></span><button class=\"sa-table-enlarge-button\" type=\"button\">Click to enlarge</button>\n</div> <p><span class=\"highlighted_text\"></span></p> <p>Based on SCHP data, the TIPS portfolio has an average weighted maturity of 7.02 years, with more than half of asset value in short-term Treasuries.</p> <figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <img alt=\"Maturity ladder\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"110\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109410143054.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109410143054.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"294\"/> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\">Maturity ladder <span>(Fidelity)</span></p></figcaption></figure><p>SCHP has an effective duration of 6.2 years, meaning a 1% change in interest rates would trigger an approximately 6.2% change in asset value, in the opposite direction. Quadratic doesn’t publicly provide IVOL’s effective duration resulting from holding SCHP and options, making interest rate risk difficult to assess.</p> <h3>Performance</h3> <p>IVOL is at -12.4% in total return from May 20, 2019 through October 22, 2026, underperforming SCHP by 4.4% annualized. Additionally, its risk metrics are much higher (maximum drawdown and volatility in the table below).</p> <div class=\"table-responsive\">\n<span class=\"sa-table-scroll-wrapper sa-hide-scrollbar\"><span data-intersection-boundary=\"start\" data-test-id=\"table-scroll-wrapper-boundary-start\"></span><table> <colgroup> <col/> <col/> <col/> <col/> <col/> </colgroup> <tr> <td> </td> <td><p>Total Return</p></td> <td><p><span class=\"j3\" id=\"jS$657\">Annual Return</span></p></td> <td><p>Drawdown</p></td> <td><p>Volatility</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>IVOL</p></td> <td><p>-12.43%</p></td> <td><p>-1.77%</p></td> <td><p>-32.39%</p></td> <td><p>8.89%</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>SCHP</p></td> <td><p>21.65%</p></td> <td><p>2.67%</p></td> <td><p>-14.26%</p></td> <td><p>5.57%</p></td> </tr> </table>\n<span data-intersection-boundary=\"end\" data-test-id=\"table-scroll-wrapper-boundary-end\"></span></span><button class=\"sa-table-enlarge-button\" type=\"button\">Click to enlarge</button>\n</div> <p><em>Data: Portfolio123</em></p> <p>The gap has widened to 9.1% over the past 12 months:</p> <div class=\"table-responsive\">\n<span class=\"sa-table-scroll-wrapper sa-hide-scrollbar\"><span data-intersection-boundary=\"start\" data-test-id=\"table-scroll-wrapper-boundary-start\"></span><table> <colgroup> <col/> <col/> <col/> <col/> </colgroup> <tr> <td><p>12 months</p></td> <td><p>Total Return</p></td> <td><p>Drawdown</p></td> <td><p>Volatility</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>IVOL</p></td> <td><p>-10.77%</p></td> <td><p>-15.07%</p></td> <td><p>4.85%</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>SCHP</p></td> <td><p>-1.65%</p></td> <td><p>-3.85%</p></td> <td><p>3.86%</p></td> </tr> </table>\n<span data-intersection-boundary=\"end\" data-test-id=\"table-scroll-wrapper-boundary-end\"></span></span><button class=\"sa-table-enlarge-button\" type=\"button\">Click to enlarge</button>\n</div> <p><span class=\"highlighted_text\"></span></p> <p>Based on share price, IVOL has lost 32.8% in asset value since inception, while SCHP is down 7.8%.</p> <figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"369\" data-og-image-facebook=\"true\" data-og-image-google_news=\"true\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"true\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"true\" data-og-image-msn=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"true\" data-width=\"1045\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412134259_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"IVOL vs SCHP, price return\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"226\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412134259_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412134259_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412134259_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412134259_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412134259_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\">IVOL vs SCHP, price return <span>(Seeking Alpha)</span></p></figcaption></figure><p>In line with the strategy description, IVOL has gained in value when the 10-year to 2-year Treasury yield spread surged in 2020 and Q1 2021. Then, it went sharply down when the spread fell, and the yield curve went inverted. It made some gains when the spread rose again, but never recovered (even including distributions), while the spread came back close to the initial value of this period.</p> <figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"465\" data-og-image-facebook=\"true\" data-og-image-google_news=\"true\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"true\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"true\" data-og-image-msn=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"true\" data-width=\"1140\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109411321313_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"10-year to 2-year Treasury yield spread\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"261\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109411321313_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109411321313_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109411321313_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109411321313_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109411321313_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\">10-year to 2-year Treasury yield spread <span>( Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, FRED database)</span></p></figcaption></figure><p>Distributions have roughly followed the asset value on its way down, with exceptional year-end payments in 2019 and 2021.</p> <figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><span><a aria-label=\"Expand image into lightbox view\" data-height=\"267\" data-og-image-facebook=\"false\" data-og-image-google_news=\"true\" data-og-image-google_plus=\"false\" data-og-image-linkedin=\"true\" data-og-image-msn=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_image_post=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_large_card=\"true\" data-og-image-twitter_small_card=\"true\" data-width=\"752\" href=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412668872_origin.png\" rel=\"lightbox\" role=\"button\"><img alt=\"IVOL distribution history\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"227\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412668872_origin.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412668872_origin.png?io=w640 640w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412668872_origin.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412668872_origin.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412668872_origin.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"640\"/></a></span><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\">IVOL distribution history <span>(Chart: author; data: Quadratic Capital)</span></p></figcaption></figure><p>Based on recent 19a notices, distributions are between 60% and 100% return of capital (“ROC”), which may be a drawback or an advantage depending on an investor’s tax situation and point of view.</p> <h3>Use Cases</h3> <p>Quadratic Capital Management proposes several use cases for IVOL:</p> <ul> <li>To hedge a fixed income or real estate portfolio against rising rates.</li> <li>To hedge an equity portfolio against market volatility.</li> <li>As a diversifier with low correlation to classic asset classes.</li> <li>To provide stable income.</li> </ul> <p>They outline the fund’s performance in stress scenarios:</p> <figure class=\"regular-img-figure\" contenteditable=\"false\"><picture> <img alt=\"Stress scenarios\" contenteditable=\"false\" height=\"215\" loading=\"lazy\" sizes=\"(max-width: 767px) calc(100vw - 36px), (max-width: 1023px) calc(100vw - 180px), 552px\" src=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412407777.png?io=w640\" srcset=\"https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412407777.png?io=w480 480w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412407777.png?io=w320 320w,https://static.seekingalpha.com/uploads/2026/10/22/2496631-17927109412407777.png?io=w240 240w\" width=\"579\"/> </picture><figcaption><p class=\"item-caption\">Stress scenarios <span>(Quadratic Capital)</span></p></figcaption></figure><p>Nonetheless, IVOL’s overall performance since its inception doesn’t impress, as reported in the previous section. Keeping part of these gains would have required timely tactical positions, or at least timely rebalances.</p> <h3>Competitors</h3> <p>IVOL’s closest competitor was the Global X Interest Rate Volatility &amp; Inflation Hedge ETF (IRVH), <a href=\"https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260916525784/en/Global-X-Announces-Fund-Liquidations\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\" title=\"https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20260916525784/en/Global-X-Announces-Fund-Liquidations\">delisted in October 2026</a>. A few other ETFs combine Treasuries with interest rate option strategies, such as the American Beacon Ionic Inflation Protection ETF (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/CPII\" title=\"American Beacon Ionic Inflation Protection ETF\">CPII</a>) and the Simplify Interest Rate Hedge ETF (<a href=\"https://seekingalpha.com/symbol/PFIX\" title=\"Simplify Interest Rate Hedge ETF\">PFIX</a>). However, they are mostly focused on inflation and lack IVOL’s specific volatility and yield curve features. The next table compares their characteristics.</p> <div class=\"table-responsive\">\n<span class=\"sa-table-scroll-wrapper sa-hide-scrollbar\"><span data-intersection-boundary=\"start\" data-test-id=\"table-scroll-wrapper-boundary-start\"></span><table> <colgroup> <col/> <col/> <col/> <col/> </colgroup> <tr> <td> </td> <td><p>IVOL</p></td> <td><p>PFIX</p></td> <td><p>CPII</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>Inception</p></td> <td><p>05/13/2019</p></td> <td><p>05/10/2021</p></td> <td><p>06/28/2022</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>Expense Ratio</p></td> <td><p>0.98%</p></td> <td><p>0.50%</p></td> <td><p>0.70%</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>AUM</p></td> <td><p>$211.96M</p></td> <td><p>$266.16M</p></td> <td><p>$11.42M</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>Average Daily Dollar Volume</p></td> <td><p>$3.35M</p></td> <td><p>$20.66M</p></td> <td><p>$24.41K</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>Dividend Yield (TTM)</p></td> <td><p>3.92%</p></td> <td><p>6.30%</p></td> <td><p>5.02%</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>Total Return*</p></td> <td><p>-25.83%</p></td> <td><p>129.50%</p></td> <td><p>17.88%</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>Annual Return*</p></td> <td><p>-6.72%</p></td> <td><p>21.32%</p></td> <td><p>3.90%</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>Drawdown*</p></td> <td><p>-29.27%</p></td> <td><p>-36.17%</p></td> <td><p>-6.38%</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>Sharpe ratio*</p></td> <td><p>-0.97</p></td> <td><p>0.71</p></td> <td><p>-0.23</p></td> </tr> <tr> <td><p>Volatility*</p></td> <td><p>10.86%</p></td> <td><p>37.84%</p></td> <td><p>4.20%</p></td> </tr> </table>\n<span data-intersection-boundary=\"end\" data-test-id=\"table-scroll-wrapper-boundary-end\"></span></span><button class=\"sa-table-enlarge-button\" type=\"button\">Click to enlarge</button>\n</div> <p><em>* Calculated with Portfolio123 from 07/5/2022 to 10/22/2026 to match inception dates.</em></p> <p>IVOL has the highest expense ratio and is behind PFIX in assets and liquidity. It is also the worst performer. PFIX has the highest return, with a note of caution: it is very volatile, its strategy is different from IVOL, and this period is too short to assess the long-term potential of these funds.</p> <h3>Takeaway</h3> <p>IVOL is a TIPS ETF with an options strategy designed to benefit from rate volatility and a steepening yield curve slope. It makes a sophisticated hedge-fund strategy available to every investor. While the theory makes a lot of sense, the fund’s history raises some questions. In particular, IVOL seems to lose asset value faster when the yield curve slope is decreasing than it gains when the slope is increasing. Although its track record is too short for a long-term assessment, other ETFs such as PFIX are more compelling as pure interest rate hedges. As a general principle of systems theory, combining different objectives increases complexity and may reduce effectiveness for each of them.</p> <ul> <li>Pros: sophisticated hedge-fund strategy, outperformance in market stress.</li> <li>Cons: high expense ratio, short/unconvincing track record.</li> </ul> <p>This article answers these three main questions about IVOL:</p> <ol> <li>How is IVOL’s portfolio structured?</li> <li>How does IVOL compare to its benchmark and competitors?</li> <li>What type of strategies is IVOL best suited for?</li> </ol> <div class=\"BFcAp\" data-test-id=\"content-container\"><div class=\"LzyJj flex sa-text-content sa-media-content sa-table-content sa-content-helpers print:block mb-32\"><div class=\"w-0 grow print:w-auto\"><div><div class=\"BFcAp\" data-test-id=\"content-container\"><div class=\"LzyJj flex sa-text-content sa-media-content sa-table-content sa-content-helpers print:block mb-32\"><div class=\"w-0 grow print:w-auto\"><div><div class=\"BFcAp\" data-test-id=\"content-container\"><p><span><em>Editor's note:</em></span><em> This article is intended to provide a general overview of the ETF for educational purposes only and, unlike other articles on Seeking Alpha, does not offer an investment opinion about the ETF.</em></p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div> </body></html>",
